# RVHyno monitor

This projects implements a monitor for the formula
```
{{formula}}
```

If you used the default options, the project is already configured and built, and it should contain the binary `monitor`.
You can run this binary as:

```sh
./monitor trace1.csv trace2.csv trace3.csv ...
```

Where `trace*.csv` are CSV files containing rows in the following format:
```
{% for name, _ in cg.args.data -%}
    {{name}}
    {%- if not loop.last -%}, {% endif -%}
{%- endfor %}
```

and the fields has these types:


|    field    |     type    |     range   |
|-------------|-------------|-------------|
{% for name, annot_ty in cg.args.data -%}
    {%- set ty, vals_range = annot_ty -%}
    | {{'%-12s'|format(name)}}| {{'%-12s'|format('`%s`'|format(ty))}}|
    {%- if vals_range -%}
    {{' %-12s'|format('∈ [%d..%d]'|format(vals_range[0], vals_range[1]))}}
    {%- else -%}
    {{'             '}}
    {%- endif -%}
    |
{% endfor %}

{% if cg.args.alphabet %}
Additionally, each field should be an element of the following alphabet: `{{cg.args.alphabet}}`.
{% endif %}

The first row of each CSV file can be a header with names of the columns (the names are ignored though, they are just for humans).

## Other options

Instead of traces, you can give the monitor a directory with traces:

```sh
./monitor directory-with-traces
```

or, actually, you can do combinations:

```sh
./monitor directory-with-traces-1 trace1.csv trace2.csv directory-with-traces-2
```

There are some runtime options that you can pass to the monitor:

|    Option              |     Description    |
|------------------------|--------------------|
| `-open-traces-limit N` | Keep at most `N` files opened at the same time |
| `-read-events-limit N` | Read at most `N` events from a file at once (then switch to another file) |


## Re-building

The monitor was originally generated with this command

```sh
{{cmd | join(' ') }}
```

These were resolved into these options and flags:
```
{{cg.args}}
```

If you did not change them, they are used when re-building the project. Below is the guide on re-configuring and
re-building.

### Re-configuring

Likely you are re-configuring the project because you want to change some options. This can be done by changing
values in `CMakeCache.txt` or `CMakeLists.txt` (see below).
After changing one of these files, just re-compile the project (see below), the re-configuration should be automatic.

If you want to re-configure manually, you can run `cmake .` from this directory. You can also choose the create a build
inside a separate directory. That can be done, for example, with these commands:

```sh
mkdir build   # create the build directory
cd build
cmake ..
```

New/altered options can be passed to CMake using the `-D` option. For example `cmake . -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=clang`.
For more details, see the documentation of CMake.

### Re-compiling

The portable way of re-building this project after any changes is
```sh
cmake --build .
```
{{ cg.args.build_system }}
{% if cg.args.build_system == 'Ninja' %}
The project was originally configured to use Ninja, so you can re-build it also by calling `ninja` from the top-level directory.
{% elif cg.args.build_system %}
The project was originally configured to use {{cg.args.build_system}}. If it was not you who picked this, try your luck
on the internet if you need help with this build system.
{% else %}
The project was originally configured to use the system's default CMake generator, which is probably "Unix Makefiles".
If that's true, you can re-build it also by calling `make` from the top-level directory.
If not, try your luck on the internet if you need help with this build system.
{% endif %}

If you want to set manually the number of cores for building, use the `-j N` parameter, where `N` is the number of processes
you want to use. For example:

```sh
cmake --build . -j 10
```
{% if cg.args.build_system == 'Ninja' %}
Note that Ninja by runs the build in parallel and picks the number of parallel processes on its own. But you can still
use `-j` to force the number of processes (e.g., if you need to run fewer processes than Ninja picks).
{% endif %}

## Changing options

You can change the options in `CMakeCache.txt` and then re-build the project. (Or you can call `cmake` with the
relevant options you want). Interesting options in variables in `CMakeCache.txt` are:

| Option            | Default |                                  Description                                         |
|-------------------|---------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `DEBUG_PRINTS`    | `OFF`   | Print debugging messages (of evaluating traces) on stderr.                           |
| `SANITIZE`        | `OFF`   | Compile with `-fsanitize=address,undefined`.                                         |
| `SANITIZE_THREADS`| `OFF`   | Compile with `-fsanitize=thread` (incompatible with `SANITIZE`).                     |
| `ENABLE_IPO`      | `ON`    | Enable interprocedural optimizations (forced OFF for Debug builds, otherwise ON).    |
| `MEASURE_CPUTIME` | `ON`    | Measure and print CPU time of running monitors.                                      |
| `RETURN_VERDICT`  | `OFF`   | Return 1 if verdict is FALSE, otherwise 0. If OFF, non-0 is returned only on errors. |
{%- if cg.args.logic == "ehl" %}
| `CACHE_ATOMS_RESULTS` | `ON`| Do not create atoms for the same traces multiple times.                              |
{% endif %}

All the options are defined in `CMakeLists-options.txt`.

## Modifying code

Feel free to modify the generated code as arbitrary. The code should be human-readable and contains a bunch (although
possibly not enough) of comments. For more information about the structure and modifications of the code,
see `HACKING.md`.
